  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // How often a goal runs by itself
  //
  // The option labels carry their own cost, because the moment to learn that
  // "every hour" is 720 model passes a month is BEFORE choosing it. A middot is
  // a title separator, which §5a permits; it is never inside a sentence.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Plan and credits
  //
  // WHAT IS NOT HERE. No dollar figure, no "one credit is X of metered model
  // spend", no allowance arithmetic. What a credit costs us is a fact about our
  // bill, and §8b makes it internal. A customer's unit is the credit.
  //
  // The numbers are VALUES and the word "credits" is a LABEL, never inside a
  // sentence with the number. That is not only brevity: a count in a sentence
  // needs a plural rule, French takes the singular below two, and every value
  // here has two decimal places, so "0,36 crédits" would be wrong on a screen
  // about money in one of six languages.
  // -------------------------------------------------------------------------
